Subject: [PATCH 0/7] bpf, x86: Introduce BPF_CGROUP_CPUID for cgroup CPUID
 virtualization

User-space applications and runtimes on x86 execute the unprivileged CPUID
instruction directly to probe CPU features, cache geometry, topology, and
vendor/model information (for example, in glibc IFUNC resolvers, language
runtimes, and cryptographic libraries).

In containerized environments, exposing raw host CPUID leaves creates
portability and isolation challenges. In particular, live-migrating
containers across hosts with heterogeneous CPU generations (e.g., via CRIU)
requires restricting applications to a common baseline feature set so they
do not bind to instructions unavailable on a migration target. Currently,
Linux provides arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_CPUID, 0) to enable hardware CPUID
faulting per thread and deliver SIGSEGV on user-space CPUID execution,
which requires each process or a user-space monitor to trap and emulate
SIGSEGV signals rather than transparently filtering CPUID at the container
boundary.

This series introduces a new x86-specific cgroup BPF attach type,
BPF_CGROUP_CPUID (reusing BPF_PROG_TYPE_CGROUP_DEVICE, exposed in libbpf as
SEC("cgroup/cpuid")), allowing cgroup BPF programs to customize or filter
user-space CPUID results for all tasks in a cgroup hierarchy.

Design overview:

- Context and return value:
  BPF programs receive struct bpf_cpuid containing read-only input
  registers (eax_in, ecx_in) and read-write output registers (eax, ebx,
  ecx, edx) pre-populated with native cpuid_count() output. Returning 1
  allows the instruction and writes the (potentially modified) output
  registers back to user space while advancing RIP past CPUID; returning 0
  denies the instruction and delivers SIGSEGV via the normal #GP(0) path.
  Hierarchical attachment (BPF_F_ALLOW_MULTI / BPF_F_ALLOW_OVERRIDE) is
  supported, with each program in the effective array observing register
  modifications made by earlier programs.

- Hardware CPUID faulting management:
  Attaching BPF_CGROUP_CPUID requires X86_FEATURE_CPUID_FAULT (Intel
  MSR_MISC_FEATURES_ENABLES or AMD MSR_K7_HWCR CpuidUserDis) and returns
  -EOPNOTSUPP otherwise. Per-CPU faulting state is shadowed in
  msr_misc_features_shadow on both Intel and AMD to avoid redundant MSR
  writes, and is synchronized across context switches, BPF attach/detach,
  and cgroup task migration (CGROUP_TASK_MIGRATED). When no
  BPF_CGROUP_CPUID programs are attached globally, context-switch checks
  are bypassed via the cgroup_bpf_enabled(CGROUP_CPUID) static key.
  Per-thread arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_CPUID, 0) (TIF_NOCPUID) retains
  precedence and continues to deliver SIGSEGV.

- Architecture scoping:
  CGROUP_CPUID is guarded by CONFIG_X86 so non-x86 architectures do not
  incur additional entries in struct cgroup_bpf.

Patch organization:
  Patch 1 avoids redundant MSR writes in set_cpuid_faulting() by tracking
          MSR_MISC_FEATURES_ENABLES_CPUID_FAULT in msr_misc_features_shadow
          on both Intel and AMD.
  Patch 2 adds the BPF_CGROUP_CPUID UAPI, verifier context access rules,
          BTF validation, and cgroup BPF execution helper.
  Patch 3 wires up x86 CPUID faulting state synchronization and #GP(0)
          user-mode CPUID trap emulation.
  Patches 4-6 sync tools UAPI headers and add libbpf and bpftool support.
  Patch 7 adds BPF selftests covering register customization, multi-program
          chaining, cgroup migration, ARCH_SET_CPUID interaction, SIGSEGV
          on deny, and detach restoration.

Andrei Vagin (7):
  x86/process: Avoid redundant MSR writes in set_cpuid_faulting()
  bpf: Add BPF_CGROUP_CPUID attach type
  x86, bpf: Wire up CPUID faulting and #GP emulation for
    BPF_CGROUP_CPUID
  tools/include: Sync uapi/linux/bpf.h for BPF_CGROUP_CPUID
  libbpf: Add support for BPF_CGROUP_CPUID
  bpftool: Add support for cgroup_cpuid attach type
  selftests/bpf: Add selftests for BPF_CGROUP_CPUID

